  "attack3_Z3_antiunitary_pairing": {
    "theorem": "Let C be unitary with C^3=1 and H commute with C. Let T be antiunitary with HT=TH, TC=CT in the semilinear sense, and T^2=C^r for r=1 or 2. If C psi=omega psi, then C(T psi)=omega^2 T psi, so T psi is an orthogonal equal-energy partner in the conjugate grading sector. The same holds with omega and omega^2 exchanged. In the neutral C=1 sector, T^2=1 and no Kramers degeneracy is forced.",
    "E8_consequence": "For any Hamiltonian obeying the certified E8 grading and one of the order-6 anti-linear symmetries CJ or C^2J, the spectra on g1 and g2 are identical and paired across the 81+81 matter sectors; no even degeneracy is forced on the neutral 86-dimensional sector.",
    "difference_from_Kramers": "Orthogonality comes from distinct C eigenvalues, not from a T^2=-1 scalar within one sector.",
